00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,873
Every constant on a control chart is derived, not

2
00:00:03,973 --> 00:00:07,604
decreed. Here is the one that sets the limits.

3
00:00:07,700 --> 00:00:13,006
Take five parts off the process. Each one lands somewhere, and sigma — the

4
00:00:13,106 --> 00:00:17,464
thing you actually want — is not one of the numbers you have.

5
00:00:17,550 --> 00:00:21,890
The range you can measure: largest minus smallest.

6
00:00:21,983 --> 00:00:25,431
Now do that again, and again. Twenty thousand

7
00:00:25,531 --> 00:00:29,059
subgroups of five, and watch their mean range.

8
00:00:29,150 --> 00:00:34,117
It settles. The mean range of n standard normals is a fixed

9
00:00:34,217 --> 00:00:38,842
multiple of sigma, and that multiple has a name: d two.

10
00:00:39,533 --> 00:00:41,998
So R bar over d two estimates

11
00:00:42,098 --> 00:00:45,889
sigma, out of ranges you genuinely measured.

12
00:00:45,983 --> 00:00:50,590
Feed it into three sigma limits, divide by root n because you

13
00:00:50,690 --> 00:00:55,915
are charting subgroup means, and gather the constants. That is A two.

14
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:01,098
At n of five, 0.5769 — the number in the AIAG table,

15
00:01:01,198 --> 00:01:07,396
